const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_ii4_80",
  "series": "Band II 4 (1701 - 1707)",
  "seq": 80,
  "text": "<p> [[265.12]] <b>80. DAMARIS LADY MASHAM AN LEIBNIZ</p>\n<p> [[265.13]] Oates, 8. (19.) August 1704. [76.90.] </b></p>\n<p> [[266.1]] S^#'r Oates 8 August 1704. </p>\n<p> [[266.2]] Whatever <Fn><i>Am Kopf der Seite von Leibniz' Hand</i>: resp.</Fn> allowances are to be made for the Language of Civilitie to Ladys, differing but\n[[266.3]] little from what would look like Flatterie to one of your own Sex; Yet I find a Pleasure in being\n[[266.4]] Prais'd by You, which I Justifie to my self from a Beleefe that when you Honour me with such\n[[266.5]] Expressions of your good opinion, You make me the Best return in Your power for that well\n[[266.6]] Grounded Esteeme I have for You, by a Desire which That has created in You of finding me\n[[266.7]] Worthy of Yours. Nor shall I ever reckon it a small matter if I have Desert enough to engage\n[[266.8]] Those of a distinguish'd Merit to wish that I had more. Thus, if I do flatter my self, I am by a\n[[266.9]] Vanitie but very little moderated, or rather Refin'd beyond that of Others, pleas'd with the\n[[266.10]] favorable things you say of me, without that Cruel allay attending this Satisfaction which a\n[[266.11]] Consciousness of not resembl'ing the Picture you have made for me must otherwise give me. </p>\n<p> [[266.12]] Whether I have rightly represented Your Systeme You Best can tell: and what you say on\n[[266.13]] that Subject I am proud of; Whilst the Inferences you would draw from thence to my advantage\n[[266.14]] can give me onely a Due acknowledgment for your wishing me so much fitter for your\n[[266.15]] Correspondence than I am. But however justly I may have express'd your Sense so far as I\n[[266.16]] indeavour'd to represent it, your Answers to some of my Enquiries makes me question whether\n[[266.17]] I fully apprehend All that is included in your Hypothesis. For I do not yet sufficiently see upon\n[[266.18]] what you ground <b>Organism's being Essential to Matter</b>: or indeed very well understand\n[[266.19]] your Meaning in these Words That Organisme is not absolutely Essential to Matter but to\n[[266.20]] Matter <b><i>arrangée par une Sagesse Souveraine</b></i>. What you would Build upon this, Forms a\n[[266.21]] very Transcendant Conception of the Divine Artifice; and such as I think could onely occur to\n[[266.22]] the thoughts of one possess'd with the highest admiration of the Wisdome of his Maker: But if\n[[266.23]] you infer the Truth of this Notion onely from its being the most Agreable one that you can\n[[266.24]] Frame to that Attribute of God, this, Singly, seemes to me not to be Concludeing: Since we can,\n[[266.25]] in my opinion, onely infer from thence that whatsoever God dos must be according to infinite\n[[266.26]] Wisdome: but are not able with our short and narrow Views to determine what the operations of\n[[266.27]] an Infinitely Wise Being must be. </p>\n<p> [[266.28]] The <b>Principle of Action</b> call'd by you <b><i>Force Primitive</i></b> is you say a <b><i>Substance</i></b>: of\n[[266.29]] the which, I still perceive not the positive Idea <b>Perception</b> being but the <b>Action</b> of this\n[[266.30]] Substance: what you add concerning its <b><i>Perceptions</i></b>, in these words, <i><b>suivant l'Analogie\n[[266.31]] qu'elle doit avoir avec notre ame</i></b>, makes me again beleeve that I do not fully understand\n[[266.32]] your scheme: since I thought before that the <b>Soul</b> and this <b><i>Force Primitive</i></b>, or <b>Principle of\n[[267.1]] Action</b>, had been the same thing. You say <b><i>renfermer les Ames dans les Dimensions\n[[267.2]] c'est Vouloir imaginer les ames comme des corps</b></i>. in regard of <b>Extension</b> this is True;\n[[267.3]] and <b>Extension</b> is to me, inseparable from the notion of All Substance. I am yet sensible That\n[[267.4]] we ought not to reject Truths because they are not Imaginable by us (where there is Ground to\n[[267.5]] admit them). But <b>Truth</b> being but the attributeing certain affections conceiv'd to belong to the\n[[267.6]] subject in question. I can by no meanes attribute any thing to a subject whereof I have no\n[[267.7]] Conception at all; as I am Conscious to my self I have not of Unextended Substance. What you\n[[267.8]] instance in therefore of <b>Lines Incommensurable</b> seemes not to me to answer the Case; for I\n[[267.9]] herein do conceive the Proposition, and have Cleare Ideas of <b>Lines Incommensurable</b>, thō I\n[[267.10]] do not see the reason of theire Incommensurabilitie: but of an <b>unextended substance</b> I have\n[[267.11]] not any Conception, from whence I can affirm, or Deny, any thing concerning it. </p>\n<p> [[267.12]] Why you think that theire is no <b>Created Substance Complete without Extension</b>:\n[[267.13]] or that the Soul (which you suppose a distinct substance) would without the Body be a\n[[267.14]] Substance Incompleat without Extension, I understand not: but my owne Beleefe that there is\n[[267.15]] no Substance whatever Unextended is (as I have alreadie said), grounded upon this that I have\n[[267.16]] no Conception of such a thing. I cannot yet but Conceive two very different Substances to be in\n[[267.17]] the Universe, thō Extension alike agrees to them Both. For I clearly conceive an Extension\n[[267.18]] without <b>Soliditie</b>, and a <b>solid Extension</b>: to some system of which last if it should be\n[[267.19]] affirm'd that God did annex <b>Thought,</b> I see no absurditie in this from there being Nothing in\n[[267.20]] <b>Extension</b> and <b>Impenetrabilitie</b> or <b>Soliditie</b>, from whence thought can <b>naturally</b>, or by\n[[267.21]] a train of Causes be deriv'd; the which I Beleeve to be Demonstrable it cannot be. But That was\n[[267.22]] never suppos'd by me; and my Question in the case would be This: Whether God could not as\n[[267.23]] conceivably by us as create an unextended Substance, and then unite it to an Extended\n[[267.24]] substance (wherin, by the way, there is methinks on your Side two difficulties for one) Whether\n[[267.25]] God, I say, could not as conceivably by us as his Doing This would be, Add (if he so Pleas'd)\n[[267.26]] the <b>Power of Thinking</b> to that Substance which has <b>Soliditie</b>. <b>Soliditie</b> and <b>Thought</b>\n[[267.27]] being both of them but attributes of some unknown Substance and I see not why it may not be\n[[267.28]] one and the same which is the common Support of Both These; there appearing to me no\n[[267.29]] contradiction in a so existence of <b>Thought</b> and <b>Soliditie</b> in the same Substance. Neither can I\n[[267.30]] apprehend it to be more Inexplicable That God should give <b>Thought</b> to a Substance which I\n[[267.31]] know not, but whereof I know some of its attributes, than to another, suppos'd, Substance of\n[[267.32]] whose very Being I have no Conception at all. and that any Substance whatsoever should have\n[[267.33]] <b>Thought</b> belonging to it, or resulting from it, otherwise than as God has Will'd it shall have so,\n[[267.34]] I cannot Apprehend. </p>\n<p> [[267.35]] That God dos in Frameing and Ordening of all his Works always make use of the most\n[[267.36]] simple meanes I doubt not, this appearing to me most sutable to his Wisdome, but whether, or\n[[267.37]] no, these simple meanes or methods are always such as Surpass not a Created intelligence, I do\n[[268.1]] not know: but am very apt to Beleeve <b>That Gods Ways are Past our finding out,</b> in this\n[[268.2]] sense. </p>\n<p> [[268.3]] I have no sooner scribled to you these Thoughts of mine, than I feare Wearying You by my\n[[268.4]] Dulness. I shall therefore Wave takeing notice of any thing more that has occur'd to me in\n[[268.5]] Considering the several parts of your letter; or makeing any such farther inquiries as perhaps\n[[268.6]] were there resolv'd, I might be able in some measure to cleare to my self. I will however now\n[[268.7]] mention to you one difficultie (as I conceive) in your Hypothesis, which I think not that I could\n[[268.8]] ever extricate it from without your assistance, and it is to me a very material one. Viz how to\n[[268.9]] reconcile your Systeme to <b>Libertie</b> or <b>Free Agencie</b>: for thō in regard of any Compulsion\n[[268.10]] from other Causes, we are according thereto <b>Free</b>, yet I see not how we can be so in respect of\n[[268.11]] the first Mover. This I omitted takeing notice of in my last not onely because I thought it too\n[[268.12]] remote an inquiry for one who wanted to be inlighten'd concerning the very foundation you\n[[268.13]] Built upon: but also because I must acknowledge that I cannot make out <b>Libertie</b> either With,\n[[268.14]] or Without any Hypothesis Whatsoever. Thō as being persuaded that I feel myself a <b>Free\n[[268.15]] Agent</b>, and that <b>Freedome to Act</b> is necessarie to our being Accountable for our Actions, I\n[[268.16]] not onelie conclude we are indu'd therewith, but am very tenacious hereof: whence I should be\n[[268.17]] sorry to find from any new Hypothesis new difficulties in maintaining of this. I think not much\n[[268.18]] that I need seek to Justifie to you the part which I own my Inclination has in this Opinion; since\n[[268.19]] what you have said in Print persuades me that you have the same Beleef with the same Bias. I\n[[268.20]] might else perhaps, alledge (in my excuse at least), that as I am a True English Woman, I cannot\n[[268.21]] but naturally have a Passion for Libertie in All senses wherein I consider it: and I would not\n[[268.22]] have so Much as the <k>Philosophy of Hanover</k> Unfavourable to any Kind of it. </p>\n<p> [[268.23]] What you write on the subject of M^#'r Locke's health, he is much oblig'd by: and I am no\n[[268.24]] less so when You recommend to me the care of one of the Best Friends I have in the World as a\n[[268.25]] matter in which you interess your self. If I could contribute to the Prolonging so Valuable a Life\n[[268.26]] as his, I should think this one of the best uses I could ever make of my own. He is not onely still\n[[268.27]] with me, but in all Probabilitie will never have health enough to permit him the thoughts of\n[[268.28]] Leaving any more a place which he has made Agreable to Others by haveing for many years\n[[268.29]] chosen to spend therein a great Part of his Time. Rational Conversation, with Mutual Good\n[[268.30]] Will, has the greatest Charmes that I know in Life, and I have hitherto been very Happy in\n[[268.31]] respect of that Enjoyment. To my Felicitie in which kind I think it a considerable addition to be\n[[268.32]] Honour'd with Your Correspondence at such a Distance, and that I am allow'd to assure you of\n[[268.33]] my being with great Esteeme </p>\n<p> [[268.34]] S^#'r Your Most Humble Servant Da Masham. </p>\n",
  "number": "80.",
  "title": "DAMARIS LADY MASHAM AN LEIBNIZ",
  "date": "Oates, 8. (19.) August 1704. [76.90.]"
};
